  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 100 AM CDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nThursday through Tuesday:\n\nA second high pressure system tracks southeastward across the \nGreat Lakes region Thursday into Friday, allowing calm and mild \nweather to persist through the end of the work week. Nocturnal\ncooling could lead to additional periods of ground fog in the\nearly morning hours of Thursday and Friday, with mostly clear\nskies and calm winds forecast. A weak easterly lake breeze is\npossible Thursday, with a synoptic (area-wide) 10 mph\nsoutheasterly breeze developing through the day Friday. As such,\nwe expect inland highs around 70 degrees Thursday and Friday,\nwith highs in the mid 60s for the east edge of the state. Dry\nweather and similar temperatures continue into the daytime hours\nof Saturday, with a south breeze as the high pressure system\ndeparts eastward. \n\nModels show fair agreement on a trough / upper low tracking east\nacross Canada this weekend, pushing out the persistent upper-level\nridging across the midwest and yielding more zonal flow. Surface\nlow pressure over the northern Great Plains on Friday lifts\nnorthward to merge with this feature into the weekend, tracking\neast across Ontario Canada and eventually dragging a SW to NE\noriented cold front across WI. We expect this cold front to reach\nWI late Saturday night into Sunday (5-15% precip chances), where\nit could either stall over central portions of the state or cross\nour region and stall just south of the state border. Some more \nsubstantial precip chances (20-40%) would then arrive Sunday night\nthrough Tuesday as a separate 500mb trough phases with the stalled\nfrontal boundary and provides some ascent. High pressure then\ntracks southeastward from Canada, crossing the Dakotas Monday\nnight into Tuesday before turning east into our region. This would\nslowly force the frontal zone away and lower precip chances, but\nalso deliver some cooler air early to mid next week. Models vary \nsubstantially on how cool things could get, but for now we're\nsticking with the middle ground of highs in the 50s and lows in\nthe 30s/40s. \n\nSheppard",
